*   **Unreimbursed Employee Expenses:** $780. Under current federal law (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), W-2 employees can no longer deduct unreimbursed work expenses on their federal returns. *(Note: California still allows this deduction on your state return if it exceeds 2% of your Adjusted Gross Income).*
Since your itemized deductions ($2,000) are far below $29,200, taking the standard deduction is much more beneficial for your federal return.

### 2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line)
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still claim certain "above-the-line" deductions (Adjustments to Income):
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,700. The IRS allows you to deduct up to **$2,500** of student loan interest per year, provided your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) is below the phase-out limit for married couples (which you appear to be well under). 

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office
Because you have freelance graphic design income ($11,200), you are considered self-employed for that portion of your earnings. You can deduct your home office expenses against this specific income, provided the space (165 sq ft) is used *exclusively and regularly* for your business. 

You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot. (165 sq ft × $5 = **$825 deduction**). This is easy and requires minimal record-keeping.
*   **Regular Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business. 165 sq ft / 690 sq ft = **~23.91%**. 
    *   Your annual rent is $28,800 ($2,400 × 12). 
    *   23.91% of $28,800 = **$6,886 deduction** just for rent. 
    *   You can also deduct 23.91% of your renter's insurance, utilities (electricity, internet), etc. 
