### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
For 2024, the **Standard Deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200**. 
To make itemizing worth it, your total itemized deductions (like charitable donations, state/local taxes capped at $10,000, and mortgage interest) would need to exceed $29,200. 
*   **Your situation:** You have $2,000 in charitable donations. Even if you max out the $10,000 State and Local Tax (SALT) deduction, you are at $12,000—well below $29,200. 
*   **Verdict:** You should almost certainly take the **Standard Deduction** on your federal return. It will save you much more money than itemizing.

### 2. What You Can Deduct (Even with the Standard Deduction)
Some deductions are "above-the-line," meaning you can take them *in addition* to the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can deduct up to **$2,500** of the $2,700 you paid, provided your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) doesn't exceed the phase-out limits for joint filers.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you have freelance graphic design income, you can deduct home office expenses against that specific income (not your W-2 income). 
    *   *Simplified Method:* 165 sq ft × $5 = **$825 deduction**.
    *   *Actual Expenses Method:* 165 sq ft / 690 sq ft = **~23.9%**. You can deduct 23.9% of your rent ($28,800/year × 23.9% = **$6,883**), plus 23.9% of utilities and renter's insurance. This method yields a much higher deduction, significantly lowering your taxable freelance income.

### 3. Unreimbursed W-2 Expenses
*   **Federal:** Under current federal law (the TCJA), W-2 employees **cannot** deduct unreimbursed work expenses ($780). 
*   **California (State):** California did not conform to this federal change. You may still be able to deduct these unreimbursed employee expenses on your California state tax return (Form 540) if you itemize deductions for the state.

### 4. How to Report Your Income
*   **W-2 ($79,400):** Reported on the main Form 1040 (Line 1).
*   **Graphic Design ($11,200):** This is self-employment income. You will report this on **Schedule C**. Your home office deduction and any other business expenses (software, internet, etc.) will be subtracted from this $11,200 to determine your net business profit. You will also owe self-employment tax (Medicare/Social Security) on the net profit.
*   **Citi Savings Interest ($1,780):** Because it is over $1,500, it must be reported on **Schedule B** (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).
*   **E*TRADE ($195):** Depending on whether this is dividend income or capital gains from selling stock, it will go on **Schedule B** (dividends) or **Schedule D** (capital gains).

### 5. Forms You Will Need (Federal)
*   **Form 1040:** The main tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule 2:** To calculate self-employment tax on your freelance income.
*   **Schedule B:** For your Citi interest and E*TRADE dividends.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $11,200 graphic design income and deduct your home office.
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate the self-employment tax on your Schedule C net profit.
*   **Form 8829:** *Expenses for Business Use of Your Home* (if you choose the "Actual Expenses" method for your home office, which looks highly beneficial for you).

**Next Steps:** Gather your W-2, 1099-INT (Citi), 1099-DIV/1099-B (E*TRADE), 1098-E (Student Loans), and your freelance income records. Tax software will easily walk you through these exact forms by asking you simple questions about the numbers above!

